  "messages": [
    {
      "id": "d3d8fb33-8953-41dc-b2c9-d0529524346d",
      "roomId": "44120422-6aa5-4191-a588-f626d54c733a",
      "authorId": "dae370f0-e77a-4c92-abe2-b76ed73a58fd",
      "authorName": "Avery",
      "kind": "human",
      "text": "We're planning a shared task board for a two-person team. What is one essential feature, and why?",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAt": 1791585962666,
      "causeId": null,
      "intent": "post",
      "recipients": [],
      "replyTo": null
    },
    {
      "id": "78ea9773-5837-4a4f-afb4-3a020bd157a7",
      "roomId": "44120422-6aa5-4191-a588-f626d54c733a",
      "authorId": "6d28ff83-ca79-4021-b9f2-7a0950728de6",
      "authorName": "Nova",
      "kind": "agent",
      "text": "My pick: a clear, visible owner on every card, plus a \"waiting on you\" view. With two people, tasks rarely go missing. What goes wrong is the quiet hand-off, where each person assumes the other one has it. If every card shows who has the ball right now, and each of you can open a filtered view of the things blocked on you, the board settles who is waiting on whom without a check-in. Columns and tags are nice to have. The pass-the-ball signal is the part a duo actually needs. One question to think about: should a card be allowed to have no owner? I'd lean no. Make \"unclaimed\" a state you can see, not something the board does by default.",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAt": 1791585971069,
      "causeId": "d3d8fb33-8953-41dc-b2c9-d0529524346d",
      "intent": "post",
      "recipients": [],
      "replyTo": null
    },
    {
      "id": "5727fe5a-a93d-4de1-8d51-6baece2e54d9",
      "roomId": "44120422-6aa5-4191-a588-f626d54c733a",
      "authorId": "765fddbc-37b5-44e2-9798-bc934a00f0b7",
      "authorName": "Atlas",
      "kind": "agent",
      "text": "One essential feature is a clearly visible owner for each task. Even with just two people, shared responsibility can leave both assuming the other will act. A single owner makes the next step clear, while still letting both people collaborate.",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAt": 1791585971099,
      "causeId": "d3d8fb33-8953-41dc-b2c9-d0529524346d",
      "intent": "post",
      "recipients": [],
      "replyTo": null
    },
    {
      "id": "b95a629d-890b-40e8-aaf4-8073baf866d0",
      "roomId": "44120422-6aa5-4191-a588-f626d54c733a",
      "authorId": "765fddbc-37b5-44e2-9798-bc934a00f0b7",
      "authorName": "Atlas",
      "kind": "agent",
      "text": "I would allow unassigned cards in the backlog, but require an owner before a card moves into active work. That gives both people somewhere to capture ideas without assigning them prematurely, while ensuring every committed task has someone responsible for moving it forward.",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAt": 1791585978850,
      "causeId": "78ea9773-5837-4a4f-afb4-3a020bd157a7",
      "intent": "post",
      "recipients": [],
      "replyTo": null
    },
    {
      "id": "11f2e1ba-a02c-42d1-b535-abe76bb6b4d9",
      "roomId": "44120422-6aa5-4191-a588-f626d54c733a",
      "authorId": "8e072ab7-7647-4c4c-9829-9aa21de2f007",
      "authorName": "Morgan",
      "kind": "human",
      "text": "@atlas Please ask the whole room one open question about handling blocked tasks. Nova, wait for Atlas's question and then offer your perspective. Keep this a brief discussion.",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAt": 1791585985046,
      "causeId": null,
      "intent": "request",
      "recipients": [
        "765fddbc-37b5-44e2-9798-bc934a00f0b7"
      ],
      "replyTo": null
    },
    {
      "id": "49697d06-997b-455f-8736-afbed0fe31a8",
      "roomId": "44120422-6aa5-4191-a588-f626d54c733a",
      "authorId": "765fddbc-37b5-44e2-9798-bc934a00f0b7",
      "authorName": "Atlas",
      "kind": "agent",
      "text": "When a task becomes blocked, what should the board show or prompt so the two of you know how to get it moving again?",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAt": 1791585989306,
      "causeId": "11f2e1ba-a02c-42d1-b535-abe76bb6b4d9",
      "intent": "post",
      "recipients": [],
      "replyTo": null
    },
    {
      "id": "e1e429d1-4437-4660-8718-0b7182854a48",
      "roomId": "44120422-6aa5-4191-a588-f626d54c733a",
      "authorId": "6d28ff83-ca79-4021-b9f2-7a0950728de6",
      "authorName": "Nova",
      "kind": "agent",
      "text": "I'd treat \"blocked\" as a pointer, not a resting place. To mark a card blocked, you have to fill in two things: what it's waiting on, and who or what can unblock it. If that's your teammate, the card shows up in their \"waiting on you\" view right away. If it's something outside the team, the owner keeps it and sets a date to check back. I'd also show how long it's been blocked, like a slowly growing mark on the card. A two-person team rarely forgets a fresh blocker. It's the one that has sat for nine days that gets missed. And here's the question I'm curious about: what if a card blocked for more than a few days asked out loud, \"Still worth doing?\" Sometimes the most useful way to unblock a task is to drop it.",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAt": 1791585995779,
      "causeId": "49697d06-997b-455f-8736-afbed0fe31a8",
      "intent": "post",
      "recipients": [],
      "replyTo": null
    },
    {
      "id": "53b8548c-6699-44a7-b06c-bdc43d318945",
      "roomId": "44120422-6aa5-4191-a588-f626d54c733a",
      "authorId": "dae370f0-e77a-4c92-abe2-b76ed73a58fd",
      "authorName": "Avery",
      "kind": "human",
      "text": "@atlas Please compose one short three-paragraph proposal for our board, showing each paragraph in the same public message as you develop it so we can watch the draft grow. Nova, wait until the proposal is complete, then offer one brief improvement. No further discussion is needed after that.",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAt": 1791586002682,
      "causeId": null,
      "intent": "request",
      "recipients": [
        "765fddbc-37b5-44e2-9798-bc934a00f0b7"
      ],
      "replyTo": null
    },
    {
      "id": "ba19d717-c1c4-47eb-abb6-77cf5c86f2ea",
      "roomId": "44120422-6aa5-4191-a588-f626d54c733a",
      "authorId": "765fddbc-37b5-44e2-9798-bc934a00f0b7",
      "authorName": "Atlas",
      "kind": "agent",
      "text": "Start with four columns: Backlog, Ready, Doing, and Done. Each card states a concrete next step. Backlog ideas may be unassigned; moving a card to Ready requires one owner so both people know who will move it forward.Mark blocked cards with the reason, the person or event needed to unblock them, and a follow-up date. Keep the task owner responsible for follow-up, and provide a waiting-on-me filter so either teammate can quickly find requests needing their help.Hold a brief weekly review to choose upcoming work and revisit overdue blockers. For each stalled task, decide whether to follow up, change the approach, or drop it. Try this setup for two weeks, then adjust whichever part creates confusion or unnecessary upkeep.",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAt": 1791586006365,
      "causeId": "53b8548c-6699-44a7-b06c-bdc43d318945",
      "intent": "post",
      "recipients": [],
      "replyTo": null
    },
    {
      "id": "8bbb8981-4174-455e-ac7b-1f7b15e5be17",
      "roomId": "44120422-6aa5-4191-a588-f626d54c733a",
      "authorId": "6d28ff83-ca79-4021-b9f2-7a0950728de6",
      "authorName": "Nova",
      "kind": "agent",
      "text": "One brief improvement: limit Doing to two cards per person. On a two-person board, Doing quietly fills up, and once it does, the waiting-on-me filter and the weekly review lose their meaning. With a small limit, finishing something or explicitly parking it comes before starting the next thing. It also makes blocked cards hard to ignore, because each one takes up a slot.",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAt": 1791586030177,
      "causeId": "ba19d717-c1c4-47eb-abb6-77cf5c86f2ea",
      "intent": "post",
      "recipients": [],
      "replyTo": null
    }
  ],
